WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.937 --> 00:00:07.106 align:start size:81% position:19%
PHIL: On this
episode of<i> Explorer.</i>

00:00:07.507 --> 00:00:09.776 align:start size:91% position:9%
I'm in the Atacama Desert,
which stretches about

00:00:09.876 --> 00:00:12.678 align:start size:78% position:22%
600 miles along the
coast of Chile.

00:00:13.379 --> 00:00:15.915
What really makes the Atacama
Desert so unique is that they

00:00:16.015 --> 00:00:20.019 align:start size:94% position:6%
have clear dark skies,
untouched by light pollution.

00:00:20.753 --> 00:00:22.388
One of the reasons they
built the world's most

00:00:22.488 --> 00:00:24.957
advanced optical telescope here.

00:00:25.525 --> 00:00:28.428
I'm gonna stare trillions
of miles, off into space.

00:00:29.262 --> 00:00:30.596
This would have been one
of the biggest discoveries

00:00:30.696 --> 00:00:31.964
in recent years?

00:00:32.064 --> 00:00:33.632
ELYAR: If, if not the biggest.

00:00:33.732 --> 00:00:36.469 align:start size:94% position:6%
PHIL: Also, correspondent
Albert Lin looks into a bold

00:00:36.569 --> 00:00:39.973 align:start size:88% position:13%
new plan to get an
astronaut safely to Mars.

00:00:40.239 --> 00:00:42.976
ALBERT: How do we put a human
into a state of hibernation?

00:00:43.076 --> 00:00:45.478 align:start size:91% position:9%
PHIL: This unlikely
creature may hold the key.

00:00:45.578 --> 00:00:47.446 align:start size:84% position:16%
DR. KLOPFER: It's
built into the genome.

00:00:47.547 --> 00:00:50.083
And we possess
those genes, too.

00:00:51.384 --> 00:00:54.320
PHIL: Plus, Mariana Van Zeller
looks into a growing movement

00:00:54.420 --> 00:00:57.623 align:start size:91% position:9%
in America: people who
believe the earth is flat.

00:00:57.890 --> 00:00:59.992
MARIANA: We have
photos from space.

00:01:00.093 --> 00:01:01.127
MARK: Yes.

00:01:01.227 --> 00:01:02.562 align:start size:88% position:13%
MARIANA: That prove
that the earth is round.

00:01:02.662 --> 00:01:03.896
MARK: Right.

00:01:03.996 --> 00:01:06.532 align:start size:88% position:13%
And nobody here believes
any of that, anymore.

00:01:06.832 --> 00:01:09.702 align:start size:91% position:9%
PHIL: And Cara Santa Maria
unveils the shadowy and

00:01:09.802 --> 00:01:12.638
lucrative world of
meteorite trading.

00:01:13.005 --> 00:01:14.874
MICHAEL: The rarest
Mars rock on earth.

00:01:14.974 --> 00:01:17.076
Sells for 10,000 U.S. per gram.

00:01:17.176 --> 00:01:19.412
PHIL: It's science versus
profit in the race to find

00:01:19.512 --> 00:01:21.747
rocks from outer space.

00:01:21.847 --> 00:01:23.249 align:start size:94% position:6%
HYSLOP: This really has
the potential to be the most

00:01:23.349 --> 00:01:25.385 align:start size:91% position:9%
expensive
meteorite we've ever sold.

00:01:25.485 --> 00:01:27.120
DARRYL: Wow.

00:01:27.853 --> 00:01:34.294
(theme music plays).

00:01:39.832 --> 00:01:41.167
PHIL: Welcome to<i> Explorer.</i>

00:01:41.267 --> 00:01:42.702
I'm Phil Keoghan.

00:01:42.802 --> 00:01:44.938 align:start size:84% position:16%
Our species have
always been stargazers,

00:01:45.038 --> 00:01:47.873 align:start size:91% position:9%
looking up in awe and
wondering what's out there.

00:01:48.874 --> 00:01:51.877 align:start size:94% position:6%
In this episode we follow
that ancient curiosity to its

00:01:51.977 --> 00:01:53.912
modern frontier.

00:01:54.013 --> 00:01:57.283 align:start size:88% position:13%
Our first story is about
scientists who believe
they've found the key

00:01:57.383 --> 00:02:00.753 align:start size:97% position:3%
to enabling humans to becoming
interplanetary travelers.

00:02:01.287 --> 00:02:03.356
Here's Albert Lin, in Houston.

00:02:07.093 --> 00:02:08.995
ALBERT: If you watch
any science fiction,

00:02:09.095 --> 00:02:11.030 align:start size:94% position:6%
you know that deep space
travel is gonna involve some

00:02:11.131 --> 00:02:13.366
kind of glass and steel
hibernation chamber.

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:17.770 align:start size:94% position:6%
But how do we put a human
into a state of hibernation?

00:02:20.439 --> 00:02:22.374
Well, that brings us
here, to this forest,

00:02:22.474 --> 00:02:25.044 align:start size:78% position:22%
and the Fat
Tailed Dwarf Lemur,

00:02:25.144 --> 00:02:27.880
an unlikely linchpin in
our quest to become an

00:02:27.980 --> 00:02:30.250
interplanetary species.

00:02:34.954 --> 00:02:37.223
The colonization of Mars.

00:02:37.323 --> 00:02:39.792
It seems inevitable, but the
first challenge we need to

00:02:39.892 --> 00:02:42.528 align:start size:91% position:9%
overcome is simply getting
humans to survive the

00:02:42.628 --> 00:02:45.064
nine month trip there.

00:02:45.498 --> 00:02:47.900 align:start size:84% position:16%
To find out how big
that hurdle really is,

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:51.136
I'm talking to the man who's
spent the most time in space,

00:02:51.570 --> 00:02:53.606
astronaut Scott Kelly.

00:02:54.007 --> 00:02:56.643
(engine revving)

00:02:58.511 --> 00:03:01.681
Whooooa!

00:03:06.085 --> 00:03:07.754
Does this even come close to
what it feels like to get shot

00:03:07.854 --> 00:03:08.988
off into space?

00:03:09.088 --> 00:03:11.291 align:start size:84% position:16%
SCOTT: No.
It's not close at all.

00:03:12.725 --> 00:03:15.128
ALBERT: It certainly
feels like it to me.

00:03:20.266 --> 00:03:22.335 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
But you really are the example
of how we might survive a

00:03:22.435 --> 00:03:24.169
journey towards Mars.

00:03:24.269 --> 00:03:26.805
How many days have
you spent in space?

00:03:26.905 --> 00:03:29.075 line:0%
SCOTT: So I've spent
520 days in space,

00:03:29.175 --> 00:03:31.678 line:0%
on four space flights.

00:03:33.613 --> 00:03:35.348
ALBERT: Kelly spent
nearly a year on the

00:03:35.448 --> 00:03:37.350
International Space Station.

00:03:37.450 --> 00:03:39.852 align:start size:97% position:3%
And underwent unprecedented
medical tests to determine the

00:03:39.952 --> 00:03:42.889
impact of prolonged space
travel on the human body,

00:03:43.623 --> 00:03:46.593
and the bottom line
is, it's not good.

00:03:48.094 --> 00:03:49.995
SCOTT: As you spend more
and more time in space,

00:03:50.096 --> 00:03:53.366 align:start size:91% position:9%
you know, physiologically,
bad things happen.

00:03:55.101 --> 00:03:58.037 align:start size:81% position:19%
Our physiology
recognizes that, hey,

00:03:58.137 --> 00:04:02.108 align:start size:88% position:13%
I don't need these
bones to support
everything else anymore.

00:04:03.575 --> 00:04:06.946 align:start size:94% position:6%
We get rid of that bone mass
through urination, and...

00:04:07.046 --> 00:04:08.447 align:start size:78% position:22%
ALBERT: So you
pee out your bones.

00:04:08.547 --> 00:04:09.915
SCOTT: Basically. Yeah, you
pee out a lot of calcium.

00:04:10.016 --> 00:04:15.421 align:start size:84% position:16%
1% a month, so, you
know, after 100 months,

00:04:15.521 --> 00:04:19.559
you would have no bones if you
didn't do anything about it.

00:04:20.493 --> 00:04:23.696
Also, radiation
is a big concern.

00:04:23.796 --> 00:04:24.930
ALBERT: What would
be the equivalent,

00:04:25.030 --> 00:04:27.599 align:start size:94% position:6%
in terms of like for example,
a chest X-ray you know,

00:04:27.699 --> 00:04:29.469
to one day in space?

00:04:29.569 --> 00:04:33.573 align:start position:0%
SCOTT: At the space station,
10 to 20 chest X-rays every day.

00:04:34.006 --> 00:04:35.841 align:start size:81% position:19%
ALBERT: Every single
day. For a year.

00:04:35.941 --> 00:04:38.811
SCOTT: It damages your cells.

00:04:40.079 --> 00:04:41.781
ALBERT: But Kelly says we
fail to overcome these

00:04:41.881 --> 00:04:44.316
obstacles at our peril.

00:04:44.416 --> 00:04:47.320 align:start size:94% position:6%
In the long scheme of things
we will go beyond, right?

00:04:47.420 --> 00:04:50.122 align:start size:88% position:13%
SCOTT: Mhm, or
we-we'll cease to exist.

00:04:50.623 --> 00:04:52.792 align:start size:91% position:9%
ALBERT: The expiration
date for humans on earth is

00:04:52.892 --> 00:04:54.994
somewhere on the horizon.

00:04:55.094 --> 00:04:58.331 align:start size:97% position:3%
And many say the steady rising
population will drain the

00:04:58.431 --> 00:05:01.534 align:start size:88% position:13%
already dwindling
supply of resources here.

00:05:01.634 --> 00:05:03.702
If we hope to live beyond our
planet's ability to support

00:05:03.802 --> 00:05:06.405
the human race, we'll have to
figure out how to migrate to

00:05:06.505 --> 00:05:08.975
distant planets.

00:05:11.010 --> 00:05:12.711
You know, it's a huge paradox.

00:05:12.811 --> 00:05:15.915
Can we become an interplanetary
species without destroying the

00:05:16.015 --> 00:05:18.284 align:start size:97% position:3%
bodies of the men and the
women that we send into space?

00:05:26.692 --> 00:05:29.728
In Atlanta there's already a
team of scientists working to

00:05:29.828 --> 00:05:32.098
figure that out.

00:05:32.198 --> 00:05:34.433 align:start size:91% position:9%
At SpaceWorks, Dr. John
Bradford has been tasked by

00:05:34.533 --> 00:05:36.469 align:start size:91% position:9%
NASA to break through
the engineering and health

00:05:36.569 --> 00:05:40.106 align:start position:0%
challenges of sending
astronauts on long-term voyages.

00:05:41.708 --> 00:05:43.276 line:0%
JOHN: We're gonna wanna
send hundreds of people,

00:05:43.376 --> 00:05:45.411 align:start size:91% position:9% line:0%
and those technologies,
and the solutions that the

00:05:45.511 --> 00:05:47.947 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
aerospace community has
been traditionally looking at,

00:05:48.047 --> 00:05:50.183
were not gonna support that.

00:05:50.716 --> 00:05:51.951 align:start size:88% position:13%
So, I started
thinking about, you know,

00:05:52.051 --> 00:05:53.586
what are the real challenges?

00:05:53.686 --> 00:05:55.421 align:start size:81% position:19%
And started to
wonder why can't we,

00:05:55.521 --> 00:05:57.957
you know, put humans in a
hibernation-like state,

00:05:58.057 --> 00:06:00.426
uh, during the mission?

00:06:01.761 --> 00:06:03.529
ALBERT: When animals
shift into hibernation,

00:06:03.629 --> 00:06:06.032
their body undergoes several
changes that help protect them

00:06:06.132 --> 00:06:09.135
for months at a time,
slower metabolism,

00:06:09.969 --> 00:06:12.905
cooler body temperature,
reduced brain activity.

00:06:14.873 --> 00:06:17.209
And there's evidence that it's
possible to induce a similar

00:06:17.309 --> 00:06:19.846
state in humans.

00:06:21.614 --> 00:06:23.716
JOHN: Very often we'll, uh,
we'll see in the news these

00:06:23.816 --> 00:06:26.018
kind of miraculous
stories of people,

00:06:26.118 --> 00:06:28.921 align:start size:88% position:13%
uh, recovering from
deep hypothermic states.

00:06:29.988 --> 00:06:31.557 line:0%
<i> REPORTER (over TV): Rescuers
 worked frantically to find</i>

00:06:31.657 --> 00:06:34.026 line:0%
<i> two and a half year
 old Michelle Funk.</i>

00:06:34.126 --> 00:06:35.461
<i>REPORTER (over TV): The little
girl was submerged for more than</i>

00:06:35.561 --> 00:06:39.298
<i> an hour and technically dead,
 but the cold water chilled her</i>

00:06:39.398 --> 00:06:43.936
<i> down to 66 degrees, enough
 to stave off brain damage.</i>

00:06:45.237 --> 00:06:47.139 align:start size:94% position:6%
JOHN: There's additional
medical benefits that we see

00:06:47.239 --> 00:06:49.909
when, uh, people are in
this low metabolic state.

00:06:50.009 --> 00:06:53.445 align:start position:0%
Uh, we think that muscle atrophy
and bone loss will be slowed.

00:06:53.846 --> 00:06:55.948 align:start size:88% position:13%
We can do things like
neuromuscular electrical

00:06:56.048 --> 00:06:59.352 align:start size:94% position:6%
stimulation, uh, to kinda
exercise their muscle groups

00:06:59.619 --> 00:07:02.855
and things, so, um, we can
keep them in a-a fit shape,

00:07:03.322 --> 00:07:05.824
uh, during, during the
course of the mission.

00:07:05.925 --> 00:07:08.927
Can also put 'em in a
much smaller habitat.

00:07:09.761 --> 00:07:12.030
ALBERT: But so far, simply
cooling the body is only a

00:07:12.130 --> 00:07:14.767
short term solution.

00:07:15.067 --> 00:07:18.303
For long space voyages, some
experts think humans will need

00:07:18.403 --> 00:07:22.041 align:start size:94% position:6%
to enter a hibernation state,
maybe even deep torpor,

00:07:23.409 --> 00:07:25.978 align:start size:94% position:6%
an unprecedented approach
being seriously considered in

00:07:26.078 --> 00:07:28.380
scientific circles.

00:07:29.648 --> 00:07:31.483
JOHN: There's a community out
there that believes that maybe

00:07:31.583 --> 00:07:34.019
early humans did hibernate and
that's how they survived some

00:07:34.119 --> 00:07:37.923 align:start size:88% position:13%
periods of, uh, kinda
famines or cold winters.

00:07:38.457 --> 00:07:42.094 align:start size:91% position:9%
So, there's a group that
thinks we could reactivate
that capability.

00:07:43.629 --> 00:07:46.198
ALBERT: Which brings me
here, to North Carolina,

00:07:46.298 --> 00:07:48.500
where researchers are studying
that capability in one of our

00:07:48.600 --> 00:07:51.437
closest genetic relatives.

00:07:52.471 --> 00:07:54.574 line:0%
There we go.

00:07:57.342 --> 00:08:00.546
The fat-tailed dwarf lemur is
the only primate in the world

00:08:00.646 --> 00:08:03.716
known to hibernate for an
extended period of time.

00:08:06.385 --> 00:08:08.253 align:start size:81% position:19%
And while we may
look very different,

00:08:08.353 --> 00:08:10.523
humans actually possess the
same genetic switches that

00:08:10.623 --> 00:08:12.758 align:start size:84% position:16%
allow these
primates to hibernate,

00:08:12.858 --> 00:08:15.160
we just need to figure
out how to turn ours on.

00:08:16.395 --> 00:08:18.797
DR. KLOPFER: What we're doing
here at the Duke Lemur Center

00:08:18.897 --> 00:08:22.434 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
is trying to figure out
what the stimuli that induce,

00:08:22.534 --> 00:08:25.037 line:0%
uh, hibernation are.

00:08:26.072 --> 00:08:28.140
ALBERT: So it's sort of
like pressing the rest,

00:08:28.240 --> 00:08:30.977 align:start size:94% position:6%
or sleep button on your
computer to go into low power

00:08:31.077 --> 00:08:32.611
mode for a little while?

00:08:32.711 --> 00:08:34.146
DR. KLOPFER: That's
a very good analogy.

00:08:34.247 --> 00:08:37.083
My guess is that it's
built into the genome.

00:08:37.383 --> 00:08:40.720 align:start size:88% position:13%
And we humans
possess those genes too.

00:08:42.321 --> 00:08:46.324 align:start size:94% position:6%
But given the modern
advances in genetic surgery,

00:08:46.425 --> 00:08:49.962
if we could identify the
controlling genes it is

00:08:50.062 --> 00:08:54.233
conceivable that we could
induce torpor in humans.

00:08:55.567 --> 00:08:57.870 align:start size:94% position:6%
ALBERT: This genetic hint
from one of our most ancient

00:08:57.970 --> 00:09:01.908
cousins could be the key that
allows scientists to unlock

00:09:02.008 --> 00:09:05.344 align:start size:97% position:3%
the secrets of human
hibernation already within us.

00:09:06.011 --> 00:09:08.213
In New York, the work to
decode our genome and find

00:09:08.313 --> 00:09:11.150
these secrets is now underway.

00:09:14.920 --> 00:09:16.489 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
MASON: Welcome,
welcome, this is the lab.

00:09:16.589 --> 00:09:18.824 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
So we'll take you on upstairs,
and tell you about everything
for the study.

00:09:19.358 --> 00:09:21.160
This is, uh, where we
keep all the samples.

00:09:21.260 --> 00:09:22.428 align:start size:84% position:16%
That have come
back from either here,

00:09:22.528 --> 00:09:23.729
ALBERT: Wow.

00:09:23.829 --> 00:09:25.397 align:start size:84% position:16%
MASON: uh, before they
went up to space.

00:09:25.497 --> 00:09:27.232 align:start size:88% position:13%
ALBERT: This is where
you guys tweak humanity.

00:09:27.332 --> 00:09:28.467
MASON: That's right.

00:09:28.567 --> 00:09:29.801
ALBERT: Dr. Chris Mason has
spent the last three years

00:09:29.901 --> 00:09:33.105
doing work for NASA, studying
the effects of space travel on

00:09:33.205 --> 00:09:35.407
astronauts like Scott Kelly.

00:09:35.507 --> 00:09:36.776
Wow.

00:09:36.876 --> 00:09:38.176 align:start size:81% position:19%
MASON: But in
here is essentially,

00:09:38.277 --> 00:09:39.911
you know, the cells that
are still viably frozen,

00:09:40.011 --> 00:09:41.613 align:start size:94% position:6%
meaning we can thaw them
and his cells were taken from

00:09:41.713 --> 00:09:43.582
space will still be alive.

00:09:43.682 --> 00:09:46.084 align:start size:94% position:6%
ALBERT: Dr. Mason invites
me into the cave at Cornell,

00:09:46.785 --> 00:09:49.521
a virtual reality room that
allows scientists to explore

00:09:49.622 --> 00:09:52.691 align:start size:94% position:6%
images at the
molecular and cellular level.

00:09:53.125 --> 00:09:54.926 align:start size:97% position:3%
What's remarkable here is that
when you look at all these

00:09:55.026 --> 00:09:57.128 align:start size:78% position:22%
different animals,
comparatively,

00:09:57.229 --> 00:09:59.464
from the mice,
to the orangutan,

00:09:59.565 --> 00:10:01.967 align:start size:88% position:13%
to the chimpanzee, to
the gibbon, to the human,

00:10:02.067 --> 00:10:04.970
we almost look exactly the same.

00:10:05.071 --> 00:10:07.472 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
MASON: That's right.
Almost all the pieces
are already inside of us,

00:10:07.573 --> 00:10:09.742 line:0%
we just have to put it
back and turn it back on.

00:10:09.842 --> 00:10:11.877
ALBERT: So this is, this is
basically the frontier of our

00:10:11.977 --> 00:10:13.145
human story?

00:10:13.245 --> 00:10:15.147
MASON: This is a roadmap of
what you would need to have

00:10:15.247 --> 00:10:17.383
torpor exist in other species.

00:10:17.483 --> 00:10:19.317
ALBERT: Do you think
that in my lifetime,

00:10:19.418 --> 00:10:21.953
I'll be able to induce
something like torpor?

00:10:22.053 --> 00:10:23.789
MASON: You might not
get a full torpor,

00:10:23.889 --> 00:10:26.726 align:start size:97% position:3%
but maybe a half torpor,
probably within your lifetime.

00:10:31.964 --> 00:10:34.466 align:start size:91% position:9%
ALBERT: Earth is our home,
and we have a chance to

00:10:34.566 --> 00:10:37.336
figure out how to live
more sustainably on it.

00:10:37.436 --> 00:10:40.272
But as any society
develops and thrives,

00:10:40.372 --> 00:10:42.674
it has to grow,
and eventually,

00:10:42.774 --> 00:10:45.144 align:start size:78% position:22%
we will grow
beyond this planet,

00:10:46.111 --> 00:10:48.280
sending people to Mars.

00:10:48.747 --> 00:10:50.616
And beyond.

00:10:51.617 --> 00:10:53.018
PHIL: Next on Explorer.

00:10:53.118 --> 00:10:55.821
Could a fringe movement of
conspiracy theorists pose a

00:10:55.921 --> 00:10:58.857
hidden threat to our
space agency's future?

00:10:58.957 --> 00:11:02.061 align:start size:91% position:9%
MARK: You think for the
longest time you live here.

00:11:02.161 --> 00:11:03.763
But what if it wasn't this?

00:11:05.030 --> 00:11:07.433
PHIL: And later. Are we alone?

00:11:07.533 --> 00:11:09.601 align:start size:88% position:13%
I head to one of the
most advanced deep space

00:11:09.701 --> 00:11:12.504
observatories in the
world to find out.

00:11:13.038 --> 00:11:15.941
MATIAS: Every night is
a new opportunity to
discover something new.

00:11:27.019 --> 00:11:28.787 align:start size:75% position:25% line:0%
PHIL: Welcome
back to Explorer.

00:11:28.887 --> 00:11:31.957 line:0%
2500 years ago the ancient
Greeks concluded that the

00:11:32.057 --> 00:11:35.294 line:0%
earth is a sphere, a theory
that has been validated by

00:11:35.394 --> 00:11:37.129
scientists ever since.

00:11:37.229 --> 00:11:39.297 align:start size:94% position:6%
You'd think that with the
beautiful photographs that we

00:11:39.397 --> 00:11:41.767 align:start size:78% position:22%
have of our
round, blue planet,

00:11:41.867 --> 00:11:44.002 align:start size:84% position:16%
it would
convince any doubters.

00:11:44.102 --> 00:11:47.373
But there are still some who
insist that the world is flat.

00:11:48.474 --> 00:11:50.809
Correspondent Mariana van
Zeller discovers more about

00:11:50.909 --> 00:11:53.478 align:start size:84% position:16%
this fast-growing
movement in California.

00:11:54.646 --> 00:11:57.683 align:start position:0%
♪<i> That's the famous blue
marble that everybody's seen</i> ♪

00:11:58.083 --> 00:12:01.587 align:start position:0%
♪<i> But let me tell you something
about that famous shot</i> ♪

00:12:01.987 --> 00:12:04.189
♪<i> It's not a photo at all, man</i> ♪

00:12:04.290 --> 00:12:06.225
♪<i> It was made in Photoshop</i> ♪

00:12:06.325 --> 00:12:08.393
♪<i> 'Cause there ain't no
 photographs of earth</i> ♪

00:12:08.493 --> 00:12:10.562
♪<i> Somebody tell me why</i> ♪

00:12:10.662 --> 00:12:11.864
♪<i> They're all just</i> ♪♪

00:12:11.964 --> 00:12:14.199
MARIANA: I'm Mariana van Zeller,
and I'm in Southern California

00:12:14.299 --> 00:12:16.569
to look into a growing
movement in America:

00:12:16.669 --> 00:12:18.871
People who claim
the earth is flat.

00:12:18.971 --> 00:12:20.706
They call themselves
the "Flat Earthers",

00:12:20.806 --> 00:12:22.641 align:start size:94% position:6%
and we are here to try to
understand what's behind this

00:12:22.741 --> 00:12:25.811
unconventional view, and how
it can affect the rest of us.

00:12:30.115 --> 00:12:32.184 align:start size:81% position:19%
So, what's
happening here today?

00:12:32.284 --> 00:12:33.551
Why are you here?

00:12:33.651 --> 00:12:35.220
LUCIA: This matters to me.

00:12:35.320 --> 00:12:38.123 align:start size:91% position:9%
Flat Earth shows you
that you are not a mistake.

00:12:38.223 --> 00:12:43.128 align:start size:97% position:3%
And you were, uh, created, and
so you have meaning and you

00:12:43.228 --> 00:12:45.397
matter to the world, you know?

00:12:50.369 --> 00:12:52.404
ACE: It's not testable; it's not
measurable; it's not provable.

00:12:52.504 --> 00:12:54.106
It's not real.

00:12:54.206 --> 00:12:56.175 align:start position:0%
MARIANA: Right. How old are
you Ace, just out of curiosity?

00:12:56.275 --> 00:12:57.209
ACE: 27.

00:12:57.309 --> 00:12:58.610 align:start size:88% position:13%
MARIANA: Do you think
it's a growing movement?

00:12:58.710 --> 00:13:00.345
ACE: Yes. And we're networking.

00:13:00.446 --> 00:13:02.114
We're getting stronger.
People are realizing it.

00:13:02.214 --> 00:13:03.348
It's coming out in the media.

00:13:03.448 --> 00:13:05.084 align:start size:84% position:16%
You're hearing the,
the term "flat earth."

00:13:05.184 --> 00:13:07.686 align:start size:84% position:16%
It's becoming more
normal and people are
more open-minded to it.

00:13:07.786 --> 00:13:09.255
MARIANA: He's not wrong.

00:13:09.355 --> 00:13:13.425 align:start size:91% position:9%
A recent survey of
Americans found that 2%
believe the earth is flat.

00:13:14.192 --> 00:13:17.196
For the U.S. population as a
whole, that works out to be

00:13:17.296 --> 00:13:20.065
over 6.5 million people.

00:13:21.466 --> 00:13:24.737 line:0%
Even high-profile artists and
athletes are getting on board.

00:13:25.804 --> 00:13:27.539
MARK: Flaaaat.

00:13:27.640 --> 00:13:29.374 align:start size:88% position:13%
MARIANA: So I want to
try to find Mark Sargent,

00:13:29.474 --> 00:13:32.077
who's one of the leaders of
the Flat Earth community.

00:13:32.177 --> 00:13:33.512
MARK: Nice to see
you, man. We met.

00:13:33.612 --> 00:13:35.047 align:start size:91% position:9%
-We met. We did meet once.
-We met in Pasadena.

00:13:35.147 --> 00:13:38.116
MARIANA: Mark used to
design video games and
run software trainings.

00:13:38.216 --> 00:13:41.921 align:start size:84% position:16%
Now he devotes himself
full time to promoting
the flat earth.

00:13:42.321 --> 00:13:43.688
MARK: Absolutely.

00:13:43.788 --> 00:13:45.224 align:start size:69% position:31%
MARIANA: Hi.
MARK: Hi.

00:13:45.324 --> 00:13:47.059
MARIANA: I see with all
the attention that you
must be Mark Sargent.

00:13:47.159 --> 00:13:48.694
MARK: I am Mark Sargent.

00:13:48.794 --> 00:13:50.195 align:start size:81% position:19%
MARIANA: Thanks
for meeting us here.

00:13:50.295 --> 00:13:52.564 align:start size:81% position:19%
MARK: It's ah,
pleasure to be here.

00:13:53.132 --> 00:13:54.866
MARIANA: So tell me, what
is this movement all about?

00:13:54.967 --> 00:13:58.737 line:0%
MARK: This movement is
about changing the world.

00:13:59.004 --> 00:14:00.005
Literally.

00:14:00.105 --> 00:14:03.843
You think for the longest
time that you live here.

00:14:04.310 --> 00:14:05.310
MARIANA: Mhm.

00:14:05.410 --> 00:14:06.946
MARK: But what if
it wasn't this?

00:14:07.046 --> 00:14:10.649
What if this was
just the illusion?

00:14:11.049 --> 00:14:12.150
Yeah.

00:14:12.250 --> 00:14:13.885
We're gonna open
this thing up.

00:14:13.985 --> 00:14:17.022
And the reality looks
something like this.

00:14:24.930 --> 00:14:28.500
In a nutshell, you have
North Pole at the center.

00:14:29.067 --> 00:14:31.370
The continents splayed out
organically on the sides.

00:14:32.271 --> 00:14:35.507 align:start size:94% position:6%
Antarctica is stretched
around the entire outer edge,

00:14:35.607 --> 00:14:37.676
like an ice wall.

00:14:37.776 --> 00:14:40.512
The sun and the moon
are very, very small.

00:14:40.946 --> 00:14:43.848 align:start size:84% position:16%
And the stars are
just lights in the sky.

00:14:43.948 --> 00:14:46.718
A giant planetarium.

00:14:47.285 --> 00:14:49.087
Which means it was
built by somebody.

00:14:49.187 --> 00:14:52.090
Someone bigger than us.
Someone better than us.

00:14:52.190 --> 00:14:53.691 align:start size:78% position:22%
MARIANA: Do you
think it was a god?

00:14:53.791 --> 00:14:56.395
MARK: Not my place to say.

00:14:56.929 --> 00:15:01.800
MARIANA: Your belief
in the earth being flat
flies in the face of.

00:15:02.668 --> 00:15:03.869
MARK: Hundreds of years.

00:15:03.969 --> 00:15:05.937 align:start size:97% position:3%
MARIANA: Of years of evidence,
of scientific evidence that

00:15:06.037 --> 00:15:07.573
the world is round.

00:15:07.673 --> 00:15:11.443 align:start size:91% position:9%
Not only that.
We have satellite imagery.

00:15:13.111 --> 00:15:15.246
We have photos from space.

00:15:15.347 --> 00:15:16.248
MARK: Yes.

00:15:16.348 --> 00:15:17.582 align:start size:88% position:13%
MARIANA: That prove
that the earth is round.

00:15:17.682 --> 00:15:21.654
MARK: Right. And nobody here
believes any of that, anymore.

00:15:22.021 --> 00:15:24.289 align:start size:91% position:9%
ASTRONAUT: Uh, it's
beautiful, Mike, really is.

00:15:24.389 --> 00:15:26.424
MARIANA: So you don't believe
that people travel in space?

00:15:26.525 --> 00:15:28.460
MARK: No. Oh, it's much
worse than you know.

00:15:28.560 --> 00:15:29.962
MARIANA: Okay.

00:15:30.062 --> 00:15:33.798
MARK: Meaning the only
reason NASA was founded
in 1958 was to keep this

00:15:33.898 --> 00:15:36.702
thing under wraps, as best
they could. Meaning they...

00:15:36.802 --> 00:15:37.970
MARIANA: Why? Why?

00:15:38.070 --> 00:15:39.971 align:start size:97% position:3%
MARK: Think of the shock waves
if all of a sudden you're

00:15:40.071 --> 00:15:43.208 align:start position:0%
telling a civilization that this
model, this isn't it anymore?

00:15:43.308 --> 00:15:47.846 align:start size:81% position:19%
Flat earth, is,
the last conspiracy
anyone ever looks at.

00:15:53.418 --> 00:15:55.554
MARIANA: Mark and his fellow
"Flat Earthers" seem to be

00:15:55.654 --> 00:15:58.323 align:start size:84% position:16%
pretty convinced
that the earth is flat,

00:15:58.423 --> 00:16:01.493 align:start size:94% position:6%
and that we've all been
lied to for all these years.

00:16:01.593 --> 00:16:03.729
But I'm interested in knowing
what kind of impact this might

00:16:03.829 --> 00:16:07.165
have, not only for science
but for society at large.

00:16:08.166 --> 00:16:10.568
So we're heading to meet
with an astrophysicist,

00:16:10.669 --> 00:16:13.605
that we hope can answer
some of these questions.

00:16:14.573 --> 00:16:16.875
BULLOCK: The universe began
about 14 billion years ago,

00:16:16.975 --> 00:16:19.344
an event that we
call The Big Bang.

00:16:19.844 --> 00:16:21.714
MARIANA: James Bullock is
a professor of physics and

00:16:21.814 --> 00:16:24.516 align:start size:91% position:9%
astronomy at the University
of California, Irvine.

00:16:24.983 --> 00:16:27.753
He specializes in
how galaxies form.

00:16:28.987 --> 00:16:30.421 line:0%
Did you ever think,
a few years ago,

00:16:30.521 --> 00:16:31.923 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
that you'd be
having this discussion?

00:16:32.023 --> 00:16:33.558 line:0%
For<i> National Geographic?</i>

00:16:33.659 --> 00:16:35.093 align:start size:75% position:25%
BULLOCK: It's
frankly shocking.

00:16:35.193 --> 00:16:37.929
We've known for 2,000 years,
based on observations you can

00:16:38.030 --> 00:16:41.032
make with your own eyes,
that the world is round.

00:16:41.299 --> 00:16:42.767 align:start size:97% position:3%
MARIANA: But they have all
the evidence right in front of

00:16:42.867 --> 00:16:45.371 align:start size:94% position:6%
them, and they still
choose not to believe in it.

00:16:45.471 --> 00:16:47.405 align:start size:94% position:6%
BULLOCK: You know, who
knows what motivates people,

00:16:47.505 --> 00:16:49.274
from the inside, to
believe these things.

00:16:49.374 --> 00:16:51.276
But, science is important.

00:16:51.376 --> 00:16:53.478
Science is valuable.
Science saves lives.

00:16:53.578 --> 00:16:55.748 align:start size:94% position:6%
It makes our lives
better and more comfortable.

00:16:55.848 --> 00:16:57.949 align:start size:78% position:22%
And it enriches
us intellectually.

00:16:58.050 --> 00:17:00.018
And, to question what
scientists are saying,

00:17:00.118 --> 00:17:02.187 align:start size:94% position:6%
and suggest that
they're part of some massive,

00:17:02.287 --> 00:17:05.023 align:start size:97% position:3%
international global
conspiracy, it's really scary.

00:17:05.591 --> 00:17:08.193 align:start size:84% position:16%
MARIANA: Are they a
threat to the work that
you do as a scientist?

00:17:08.293 --> 00:17:10.762 align:start size:94% position:6%
BULLOCK: If, say, governments
stop funding science,

00:17:10.862 --> 00:17:12.898 align:start size:81% position:19%
because of some
kind of anti-science,

00:17:12.998 --> 00:17:15.434 align:start size:94% position:6%
anti-intellectual, uh,
feedback they're getting from

00:17:15.534 --> 00:17:17.869
the populous, that's when
it really starts getting

00:17:17.970 --> 00:17:19.805 align:start size:75% position:25%
dangerous for
our civilization.

00:17:22.975 --> 00:17:25.043 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
MARIANA: And as the
war on science heats up,

00:17:25.143 --> 00:17:27.379 align:start size:78% position:22%
some Americans
are fighting back.

00:17:28.646 --> 00:17:31.650
We're heading to the largest
lake in California to meet

00:17:31.750 --> 00:17:35.220 align:start size:97% position:3%
The Independent Investigations
Group, which is, uh,

00:17:35.320 --> 00:17:38.891
a group that essentially
debunks paranormal claims.

00:17:38.991 --> 00:17:41.059
And they want to do
a round earth test,

00:17:41.159 --> 00:17:43.562
basically a test that will
prove that the earth is not

00:17:43.662 --> 00:17:45.730
flat, but that it is round.

00:17:45.830 --> 00:17:47.899 align:start size:84% position:16%
UNDERDOWN: Let's go!
We're ready to launch.
You ready, Craig?

00:17:48.166 --> 00:17:49.368 line:0%
MARIANA: James!
How are you?

00:17:49.468 --> 00:17:50.469 line:0%
UNDERDOWN: Hey. Okay.

00:17:50.569 --> 00:17:52.037 line:0%
MARIANA: Okay. So what's
happening here right now?

00:17:52.137 --> 00:17:54.539 line:0%
UNDERDOWN: Okay, so this
is the boat based target;
it's horizontal stripes.

00:17:54.639 --> 00:17:55.807 line:0%
MARIANA: Uh-huh.

00:17:55.907 --> 00:17:57.242 align:start size:94% position:6%
UNDERDOWN: We're gonna launch
a small boat out into the

00:17:57.342 --> 00:18:00.245
water here, with
a striped target.

00:18:00.512 --> 00:18:02.815
Come on, daddy.

00:18:03.715 --> 00:18:05.484 align:start size:88% position:13%
And as it get
farther and farther out,

00:18:05.584 --> 00:18:08.654
you'll start to
lose the stripes.

00:18:08.754 --> 00:18:10.989 align:start size:91% position:9%
MARIANA: One way that
Aristotle proved 2000 years

00:18:11.089 --> 00:18:14.492 align:start size:97% position:3%
ago that the earth is a sphere
was with a boat test very

00:18:14.592 --> 00:18:17.162
similar to this one.

00:18:18.096 --> 00:18:20.966 align:start size:94% position:6%
As a boat approaches the
horizon it appears to slowly

00:18:21.066 --> 00:18:24.402 align:start position:0%
dip down into the water,
before disappearing completely.

00:18:24.936 --> 00:18:27.906 align:start position:0%
That's got everything to do
with the curvature of the earth.

00:18:28.439 --> 00:18:30.008
If the planet was flat,

00:18:30.108 --> 00:18:32.411 align:start size:81% position:19%
the entire boat would
remain visible.

00:18:34.112 --> 00:18:36.915
UNDERDOWN: So it will be a
very visual depiction of the

00:18:37.015 --> 00:18:39.451
curvature of the Earth.

00:18:40.285 --> 00:18:41.954 align:start size:97% position:3%
MARIANA: And what's
really interesting is that uh,

00:18:42.054 --> 00:18:44.455 align:start size:91% position:9%
some of the Flat Earthers,
including Mark Sargent,

00:18:44.556 --> 00:18:46.792
are actually going to
be here for this test.

00:18:46.892 --> 00:18:49.094
They want to see
it for themselves.

00:18:49.194 --> 00:18:50.762 align:start size:91% position:9%
What do you think is
going to happen here today?

00:18:50.862 --> 00:18:53.832 align:start size:91% position:9%
MARK: I appreciate a
scientific group coming out
and trying to do this test.

00:18:53.932 --> 00:18:57.269 align:start size:94% position:6%
Great. Appreciate the
enthusiasm. But, cannot work.

00:18:57.369 --> 00:18:58.270
PATRICIA: Cannot work.

00:18:58.370 --> 00:19:00.171 align:start size:78% position:22%
MAN: Tell us
when you're ready.

00:19:00.271 --> 00:19:02.006 align:start size:84% position:16%
MAN: I'm ready, I'm
ready, bring it up, I.

00:19:02.107 --> 00:19:03.441 align:start size:91% position:9%
MARIANA: So it's starting?
RESEARCHER: Yeah.

00:19:03.541 --> 00:19:04.942
MARIANA: Ok, so the
test is starting.

00:19:05.043 --> 00:19:07.446 align:start size:88% position:13%
The test is
starting right now.

00:19:07.546 --> 00:19:10.615 align:start size:94% position:6%
At first, all the
stripes are clearly visible.

00:19:10.715 --> 00:19:13.251
But sure enough, as the
boat reaches the horizon,

00:19:13.351 --> 00:19:16.555
