giant studio set,

00:20:15.513 --> 00:20:18.350
uh, all the world's a
stage and you're on it.

00:20:18.450 --> 00:20:19.784 align:start size:88% position:13%
MARIANA: I still
don't understand though.

00:20:19.885 --> 00:20:22.053 align:start size:84% position:16%
Like, who's gaining
from all this, from
this large conspiracy?

00:20:22.153 --> 00:20:24.789 align:start size:94% position:6%
MARK: The highest authority,
higher than presidents.

00:20:24.889 --> 00:20:26.792
Uh, the super rich.

00:20:26.892 --> 00:20:28.860 align:start size:78% position:22%
People that are
behind the scenes.

00:20:28.961 --> 00:20:31.029
Remember, the first rule
of power is stay hidden.

00:20:31.129 --> 00:20:32.530
MARIANA: So this is for me
where I think it gets really

00:20:32.630 --> 00:20:35.267 align:start size:97% position:3%
dangerous, because we're
going back into the Dark Ages.

00:20:35.367 --> 00:20:36.501
Right?
MARK: Mm.

00:20:36.601 --> 00:20:38.703
MARIANA: You're essentially
perpetuating ignorance by

00:20:38.803 --> 00:20:40.605
denying science.

00:20:40.705 --> 00:20:42.774
You know, there's a real-life
implication in all of this.

00:20:42.874 --> 00:20:44.509
MARK: Science has
had its chance,

00:20:44.609 --> 00:20:46.878 align:start size:81% position:19%
and they aren't
putting up a defense.

00:20:46.978 --> 00:20:51.116
I think that Flat Earth
could potentially usher
in a new Golden Age.

00:20:51.483 --> 00:20:55.254
Or, it could usher
in an age of chaos.

00:21:00.925 --> 00:21:02.960
MARIANA: It's easy to
dismiss this as just a,

00:21:03.061 --> 00:21:05.397
a silly theory,
and unimportant.

00:21:07.232 --> 00:21:09.801
But the problem is that it's a
growing movement in America.

00:21:09.901 --> 00:21:13.538 align:start size:84% position:16%
More and more young
people seem to believe
that the world is flat.

00:21:14.739 --> 00:21:17.875
And, you know, it's more than
just distrusting government.

00:21:17.976 --> 00:21:20.712
It's this notion that
thousands of years of

00:21:20.812 --> 00:21:24.282
empirical scientific evidence
is now being dismissed as a

00:21:24.382 --> 00:21:26.150
mere conspiracy.

00:21:26.251 --> 00:21:28.453
And that's where I think
it really starts getting

00:21:28.553 --> 00:21:30.722
dangerous, for all of us.

00:21:34.258 --> 00:21:37.995
PHIL: When we come back, does
life exist on distant planets?

00:21:38.830 --> 00:21:40.999
I travel to one of the
most otherworldly places

00:21:41.099 --> 00:21:42.667
to find out.

00:21:44.102 --> 00:21:44.969 align:start size:91% position:9%
So this would've been one
of the biggest discoveries
in recent years?

00:21:45.069 --> 00:21:47.506
EYLAR: For sure.
If not the biggest.

00:21:48.272 --> 00:21:49.674
PHIL: And later.

00:21:49.774 --> 00:21:51.510
When a meteorite
strikes the earth,

00:21:51.610 --> 00:21:56.014 align:start size:88% position:13%
is it scientists or
deep-pocketed collectors
that get first dibs?

00:21:56.781 --> 00:21:58.684
HYSLOP: I have heard stories
of meteorites changing hands

00:21:58.784 --> 00:22:01.053
for $1 million.

00:22:10.195 --> 00:22:13.097 align:start size:91% position:9% line:0%
PHIL: Most of us live
under light-polluted skies.

00:22:13.198 --> 00:22:17.769 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
The best stargazing
spots on earth are
often extremely remote.

00:22:18.669 --> 00:22:21.106 align:start size:88% position:13%
Places like the
Atacama Desert in Chile,

00:22:21.206 --> 00:22:24.609
where scientists have an
unparalleled view of deep

00:22:24.709 --> 00:22:28.013 align:start size:94% position:6%
space, and are making
discoveries that are changing

00:22:28.113 --> 00:22:30.581
our understandings
of the universe.

00:22:31.115 --> 00:22:33.752 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
Our next story is my
journey to see stars up close,

00:22:33.852 --> 00:22:36.054 line:0%
as close as you can get.

00:22:39.524 --> 00:22:42.560
Rising more than 8,000
feet above sea level,

00:22:42.660 --> 00:22:46.398 align:start size:91% position:9%
the Atacama is the highest,
driest desert on earth.

00:22:50.368 --> 00:22:53.204 align:start size:97% position:3%
There are places here where
it's so dry that they've never

00:22:53.304 --> 00:22:55.874
had a drop of rain.

00:22:59.410 --> 00:23:02.814 align:start size:81% position:19%
The soil has no
water, or nutrients.

00:23:04.582 --> 00:23:07.051
But what really makes the
Atacama Desert so unique is

00:23:07.151 --> 00:23:09.620 align:start size:78% position:22%
that they have
clear, dark skies,

00:23:09.720 --> 00:23:12.424
untouched by light pollution.

00:23:12.991 --> 00:23:15.226 align:start size:94% position:6%
One of the reasons they built
the world's most advanced

00:23:15.326 --> 00:23:18.062
optical telescope here.

00:23:18.162 --> 00:23:20.531 align:start size:84% position:16%
If you're a star
gazer, worth the trip.

00:23:20.632 --> 00:23:23.802
The Paranal Observatory:
it's as good as it gets.

00:23:28.006 --> 00:23:31.276
This telescope tell me,
why is it so special?

00:23:31.376 --> 00:23:33.979 align:start size:84% position:16%
MATIAS: The size of
the main mirror is very
important, because.

00:23:34.079 --> 00:23:35.646 align:start size:78% position:22% line:0%
PHIL: How, how
big are we talking?

00:23:35.746 --> 00:23:38.049 align:start size:81% position:19% line:0%
MATIAS: 8.2
meters in diameters.

00:23:38.149 --> 00:23:40.051
PHIL: So maybe 24, 25 feet?

00:23:40.151 --> 00:23:42.554 align:start size:84% position:16%
MATIAS: The larger the
telescope, the more
light you collect.

00:23:42.654 --> 00:23:44.021
PHIL: Oh! It's moving.

00:23:44.121 --> 00:23:45.156
MATIAS: Yeah, it's moving.

00:23:45.257 --> 00:23:46.390 align:start size:78% position:22%
PHIL: Oh.
MATIAS: It's fine.

00:23:46.490 --> 00:23:49.160 align:start size:94% position:6%
PHIL: Matias, can we walk
up so we can see the mirror?

00:23:49.260 --> 00:23:50.328
MATIAS: Of course.

00:23:50.428 --> 00:23:52.264 align:start size:97% position:3%
PHIL: Can we go up the stairs?
MATIAS: Yeah, yeah.

00:23:52.364 --> 00:23:54.266 align:start size:88% position:13%
PHIL: So here you
really get to see this...

00:23:54.366 --> 00:23:56.167
MATIAS: Exactly.
PHIL: This mirror!

00:23:56.267 --> 00:23:57.401
MATIAS: Yeah.

00:23:57.501 --> 00:23:59.503 align:start size:97% position:3%
PHIL: The VLT's largest
mirror collects the light from

00:23:59.603 --> 00:24:01.439
distant stars and planets.

00:24:01.539 --> 00:24:04.342 align:start size:94% position:6%
From there, a series of
smaller mirrors reflect that

00:24:04.442 --> 00:24:07.345 align:start size:97% position:3%
light onto one of
several instruments that allow

00:24:07.445 --> 00:24:10.014 align:start size:78% position:22%
astronomers to
study the subject.

00:24:10.114 --> 00:24:12.183
How thick is this big one?

00:24:12.283 --> 00:24:13.851
MATIAS: 17 centimeters.

00:24:13.951 --> 00:24:15.987 align:start size:91% position:9%
PHIL: So, we're
looking at about 7 inches,

00:24:16.087 --> 00:24:17.355
or something like that, right.

00:24:17.455 --> 00:24:18.656 align:start size:81% position:19%
MATIAS: Yeah,
something like that.

00:24:18.756 --> 00:24:20.992
It weighs more than 20 tons,
so it's a big piece of glass.

00:24:21.092 --> 00:24:22.927 align:start size:88% position:13%
PHIL: Wow.
MATIAS: It's like. Right.

00:24:23.628 --> 00:24:25.930
PHIL: But having one of the
largest mirrors in the world

00:24:26.030 --> 00:24:28.867
is useless unless
you can focus it.

00:24:28.967 --> 00:24:31.869
That's why you need lasers.

00:24:31.969 --> 00:24:33.771
Just like something
out of<i> Star Wars,</i>

00:24:33.871 --> 00:24:36.441 align:start size:97% position:3%
a powerful laser beam
shoots out from the telescope,

00:24:36.541 --> 00:24:39.544
causing sodium atoms high
in the atmosphere to glow,

00:24:39.644 --> 00:24:42.680
creating an artificial star
that allows the scientists to

00:24:42.780 --> 00:24:45.550
calibrate the VLT.

00:24:46.618 --> 00:24:48.119 align:start size:88% position:13%
MATIAS: I mean, this
is state of the art, uh,

00:24:48.219 --> 00:24:49.788
instrumentation in astronomy,

00:24:49.888 --> 00:24:51.856
so it's, it's, this
is the right place,

00:24:51.956 --> 00:24:53.958
uh, if you want to do.

00:24:54.059 --> 00:24:55.693 align:start size:91% position:9%
PHIL: The work that you do.
MATIAS: Exactly.

00:24:55.793 --> 00:24:58.062
There will be also very
nice, new instrument.

00:24:58.162 --> 00:24:59.197
PHIL: I love that.

00:24:59.297 --> 00:25:01.633 align:start size:91% position:9%
Look, the lights are
going down, Matias, right?

00:25:02.734 --> 00:25:05.303
MATIAS: Exactly. Look.

00:25:06.371 --> 00:25:08.940 align:start size:97% position:3%
This is part of the excitement
of working here, right?

00:25:09.040 --> 00:25:11.109
Every night is a
new opportunity to,

00:25:11.209 --> 00:25:13.812 align:start size:88% position:13%
to discover
something, something new.

00:25:17.916 --> 00:25:20.351 align:start size:91% position:9%
PHIL: The half-billion
dollar facility is the most

00:25:20.451 --> 00:25:23.488
productive ground-based
observatory in the world,

00:25:23.588 --> 00:25:25.389 align:start size:88% position:13%
advancing our
knowledge of black holes,

00:25:25.489 --> 00:25:28.260
and how stars and
planets are born.

00:25:29.694 --> 00:25:32.497
In 2008, the very large
telescope detected the

00:25:32.597 --> 00:25:35.933 align:start size:97% position:3%
afterglow of a gamma ray burst
among the farthest objects

00:25:36.033 --> 00:25:37.969
ever observed.

00:25:38.069 --> 00:25:40.171
Near the very edge of
the known universe,

00:25:40.271 --> 00:25:43.508
light from this explosion took
almost 13 billion years to get

00:25:43.608 --> 00:25:47.379
here, giving us a window into
the universe in its infancy.

00:25:48.513 --> 00:25:50.481
MATIAS: We can go to
the, um, control room.

00:25:50.582 --> 00:25:51.683
PHIL: Mhm.

00:25:51.783 --> 00:25:54.019
MATIAS: Where we
operate, remotely.

00:25:55.687 --> 00:25:58.022
PHIL: But perhaps the most
significant work done here is

00:25:58.122 --> 00:26:01.292 align:start size:84% position:16%
the discovery and
studying of exoplanets,

00:26:01.892 --> 00:26:04.963
planets outside
our solar system.

00:26:09.166 --> 00:26:10.068
MATIAS: Okay.

00:26:10.168 --> 00:26:12.269
So here, we are in
uh, the control room.

00:26:12.369 --> 00:26:14.906
PHIL: And each, so each one of
these are control centers for?

00:26:15.006 --> 00:26:18.576 align:start position:0%
MATIAS: For, for the instrument
on that specific telescope.

00:26:19.444 --> 00:26:22.113 align:start size:94% position:6%
PHIL: Observation time on
these powerful telescopes is

00:26:22.213 --> 00:26:25.083 align:start size:94% position:6%
precious, and requests
come from all over the world.

00:26:25.183 --> 00:26:29.220 align:start size:97% position:3%
Only astronomers with the best
proposals get to use them.

00:26:32.557 --> 00:26:35.293 align:start size:88% position:13%
Elyar is an
astronomer from Iran and,

00:26:35.393 --> 00:26:38.963
uh, I guess this is a great
way be able to sort of get a

00:26:39.063 --> 00:26:41.466 align:start size:81% position:19%
sense of what an
exoplanet looks like.

00:26:41.566 --> 00:26:42.701
ELYAR: Yes. For sure.

00:26:42.801 --> 00:26:45.470 align:start size:91% position:9%
PHIL: These images are
really quite extraordinary.

00:26:45.570 --> 00:26:48.640 align:start size:84% position:16%
We've known about
exoplanets since 1992,

00:26:48.740 --> 00:26:51.642 align:start size:88% position:13%
but didn't catch an
image of one until 2004,

00:26:51.743 --> 00:26:54.646
when the team here at
Paranal made history.

00:26:56.047 --> 00:26:58.649 align:start size:84% position:16%
ELYAR: Here, we
actually have a direct
image of an exoplanet.

00:26:58.750 --> 00:27:01.653
This is the star,
and this is a,

00:27:01.753 --> 00:27:04.522 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
uh, giant planet, it's,
uh, a little bit bigger than

00:27:04.622 --> 00:27:06.357 align:start size:78% position:22% line:0%
Jupiter, around
that star that we,

00:27:06.457 --> 00:27:08.726 line:0%
we could take its
picture directly.

00:27:08.826 --> 00:27:10.528
PHIL: It is the first-ever
image of a planet outside

00:27:10.628 --> 00:27:12.363
our solar system.

00:27:12.463 --> 00:27:17.067 align:start size:84% position:16%
This exoplanet orbits a
star located 230 light
years from earth,

00:27:17.468 --> 00:27:21.272 align:start position:0%
which sounds far, unless you're
talking to an astronomer.

00:27:21.673 --> 00:27:23.575 align:start size:78% position:22%
ELYAR: It's in
our neighborhoods,

00:27:23.675 --> 00:27:25.977
in terms of the size
of the, uh, galaxy,

00:27:26.077 --> 00:27:28.279 align:start size:91% position:9%
because our galaxy's about
100,000 light years.

00:27:28.380 --> 00:27:31.316
PHIL: Since the imaging
technology at Paranal is

00:27:31.416 --> 00:27:33.818
relatively new and
not widely available,

00:27:33.918 --> 00:27:36.988
these so-called neighborhoods
are still largely unexplored.

00:27:37.655 --> 00:27:40.225 align:start size:94% position:6%
But recently, the astronomers
here happened upon a

00:27:40.325 --> 00:27:43.261 align:start size:91% position:9%
potentially
earth-shattering discovery.

00:27:44.029 --> 00:27:46.063
ELYAR: So this is a,
this planetary system,

00:27:46.163 --> 00:27:47.999
is covered around a
star called Trappist.

00:27:48.099 --> 00:27:49.600
Trappist-1.

00:27:49.701 --> 00:27:52.436
And they found 7 rocky
planets around this star.

00:27:52.837 --> 00:27:54.605 align:start size:88% position:13%
PHIL: And what do we
know about these planets?

00:27:54.705 --> 00:27:57.275
ELYAR: What we know is that 3
of them are for sure in the

00:27:57.375 --> 00:28:00.111
habitable zone of the star,
which means that there is a

00:28:00.211 --> 00:28:03.214 align:start size:97% position:3%
possibility that there is
liquid water on their surface.

00:28:07.618 --> 00:28:10.755 align:start size:94% position:6%
PHIL: So are you now
zeroing in on these planets,

00:28:10.855 --> 00:28:13.858 align:start size:97% position:3%
and starting to really
understand them more and more?

00:28:13.958 --> 00:28:16.527
ELYAR: For sure. I mean,
we are really lucky that,

00:28:16.627 --> 00:28:19.531
from our point of view, all 7
of these planets pass in front

00:28:19.631 --> 00:28:21.700
of the, the host star.

00:28:21.800 --> 00:28:24.268 align:start size:91% position:9%
So, as it, as it passes
in front of the host star,

00:28:24.368 --> 00:28:27.304
we can actually study,
study their atmosphere.

00:28:28.138 --> 00:28:30.008 align:start size:94% position:6%
PHIL: I learn that an
exoplanet passing in front of

00:28:30.108 --> 00:28:33.544
its star is a very important
time for these astronomers.

00:28:34.044 --> 00:28:36.547 line:0%
For one, it slightly dims
the light from the star,

00:28:36.647 --> 00:28:39.484 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
making it the best time
to detect the planet in
the first place.

00:28:39.984 --> 00:28:43.687 line:0%
Also, as light from the
star passes through the
planet's atmosphere,

00:28:43.788 --> 00:28:46.891
astronomers can identify
which gases are present.

00:28:47.992 --> 00:28:50.728 align:start size:94% position:6%
And by then studying the
composition of all the gases

00:28:50.828 --> 00:28:53.731
you start to understand more
about what the potential is

00:28:53.831 --> 00:28:55.399
for life, that sort of thing?

00:28:55.499 --> 00:28:56.734
ELYAR: Absolutely, yes.

00:28:56.834 --> 00:28:58.570 align:start size:88% position:13%
PHIL: And your
personal feeling is what?

00:28:58.670 --> 00:29:00.705 align:start size:88% position:13%
Maybe there is some life
there? In Trappist?

00:29:00.805 --> 00:29:03.841
ELYAR: If I had to bet, I
would say probably not.

00:29:03.941 --> 00:29:07.645
The reason that I say probably
not is because this star emits

00:29:07.745 --> 00:29:09.814
a lot of X-ray radiation.

00:29:09.914 --> 00:29:12.483 align:start size:88% position:13%
As we know, X-ray is
not quite good for life.

00:29:12.583 --> 00:29:14.285
But, you never know.

00:29:14.385 --> 00:29:16.921 align:start size:97% position:3%
We, we have, I mean, we
only know one example of life,

00:29:17.021 --> 00:29:19.323 align:start size:88% position:13%
uh, that has
evolved in the universe.

00:29:19.423 --> 00:29:21.659
So we are very inexperienced.

00:29:21.759 --> 00:29:23.161
PHIL: You have dreams, Matias?

00:29:23.261 --> 00:29:25.629
You have something that
you're searching for?

00:29:25.730 --> 00:29:28.165 align:start size:75% position:25%
You feel like
you want to find?

00:29:28.265 --> 00:29:33.337
MATIAS: If we find
sign of life, in,

00:29:33.437 --> 00:29:37.675
outside the solar system,
would be, I mean, that,

00:29:37.775 --> 00:29:40.678
I think that is one of
the biggest question
of, of mankind, right?

00:29:40.778 --> 00:29:41.979
PHIL: Yeah.

00:29:42.079 --> 00:29:43.515 align:start size:84% position:16%
MATIAS: Are we
alone in the universe?

00:29:43.615 --> 00:29:45.983
And probably the answer to
this will be provided by

00:29:46.084 --> 00:29:48.352
astronomy in the
coming decades,

00:29:48.452 --> 00:29:52.524
so, it's challenging, but
it's also super exciting.

00:29:54.291 --> 00:29:55.860
PHIL: The high-powered
telescopes here give

00:29:55.960 --> 00:29:58.663 align:start size:94% position:6%
astronomers a groundbreaking
view of deep space.

00:29:58.763 --> 00:30:01.032 align:start size:94% position:6%
But the remote desert
landscape on which they stand

00:30:01.132 --> 00:30:04.402 align:start size:94% position:6%
provides a truly
incredible look at the stars,

00:30:05.636 --> 00:30:08.539
with or without a telescope.

00:30:09.473 --> 00:30:13.544 align:start size:84% position:16%
For me, I've just never
seen the sky like this
in my entire life.

00:30:13.811 --> 00:30:15.946
ELYAR: You can see this,
this brilliant band of,

00:30:16.046 --> 00:30:17.615
uh, light across the sky.

00:30:17.715 --> 00:30:20.051 align:start size:94% position:6%
That, as, as you can see
just goes all the way across.

00:30:20.151 --> 00:30:21.886 align:start size:88% position:13%
PHIL: Beautiful.
And that band is called.

00:30:21.986 --> 00:30:23.521
ELYAR: That's the Milky Way.
PHIL: That's the Milky Way.

00:30:23.621 --> 00:30:25.389
ELYAR: That's our home.
That's our home galaxy.

00:30:25.489 --> 00:30:27.391
So if we can, I would, I would
like to actually have you come

00:30:27.491 --> 00:30:29.560 align:start position:0%
a bit closer to home and
actually have a look at Jupiter.

00:30:29.660 --> 00:30:31.229
PHIL: Okay. I like Jupiter.
ELYAR: If, if you would like.

00:30:31.329 --> 00:30:33.430
So that's the brightest star
that you can see in the sky.

00:30:33.530 --> 00:30:34.698
PHIL: Oh, there.

00:30:34.799 --> 00:30:35.900
ELYAR: Yeah, the
one right there.

00:30:36.000 --> 00:30:37.569 align:start size:84% position:16%
PHIL: I see. Okay.
ELYAR: Now you can see.

00:30:37.669 --> 00:30:39.036 align:start size:78% position:22%
PHIL: Oh, wow.
ELYAR: You have it.

00:30:39.136 --> 00:30:40.705
PHIL: Yeah, that's beautiful.

00:30:40.805 --> 00:30:42.740 align:start size:78% position:22%
ELYAR: And you
also see four dots,

00:30:42.840 --> 00:30:45.810 align:start size:97% position:3%
uh, very close to Jupiter,
and those are Jupiter's moons.

00:30:47.145 --> 00:30:48.579 align:start size:81% position:19%
PHIL: I really
appreciate the tour.

00:30:48.679 --> 00:30:51.014
ELYAR: It was my pleasure.

00:30:51.448 --> 00:30:53.050 align:start size:94% position:6%
PHIL: This
state-of-the-art observatory,

00:30:53.151 --> 00:30:55.086 align:start size:88% position:13%
and the men and women
who work here, are on the

00:30:55.186 --> 00:30:57.988
vanguard of an amazing
explosion of knowledge.

00:30:58.422 --> 00:31:00.758 align:start size:81% position:19%
And even with all
that's been explored,

00:31:00.859 --> 00:31:03.127
we've only scratched the
surface of the historic

00:31:03.227 --> 00:31:06.197
discoveries that await
us on distant horizons.

00:31:08.332 --> 00:31:10.134
When we come back.

00:31:10.234 --> 00:31:12.570
CARA: Here in Morocco when a
meteorite strikes the Earth,

00:31:12.670 --> 00:31:14.972 align:start size:81% position:19%
there's a rush to
find the best pieces.

00:31:15.439 --> 00:31:18.176 align:start size:91% position:9%
MICHAEL: Just those two
pieces, they want $20,000.

00:31:18.276 --> 00:31:20.010 align:start size:94% position:6%
CARA: $20,000 for
what's in my hand right now?

00:31:20.110 --> 00:31:21.512
MICHAEL: Yes.

00:31:28.852 --> 00:31:30.288 line:0%
PHIL: Welcome back.

00:31:30.388 --> 00:31:32.423 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
While space might
appear like an empty vacuum,

00:31:32.523 --> 00:31:35.493 line:0%
it is in fact filled
with cosmic debris,

00:31:35.593 --> 00:31:37.829 align:start size:81% position:19%
like asteroids,
comets, and meteors.

00:31:37.929 --> 00:31:41.499 align:start size:91% position:9%
Tens of thousands of
these meteors become fiery

00:31:41.599 --> 00:31:44.602
meteorites, breaking
through our atmosphere

00:31:44.702 --> 00:31:46.938
before crashing down on earth.

00:31:47.038 --> 00:31:51.376 align:start size:91% position:9%
These extraterrestrial
rocks can offer rich
insight into alien worlds,

00:31:51.476 --> 00:31:55.045 align:start size:94% position:6%
and are often so valuable
they're worth more than gold.

00:31:55.946 --> 00:31:58.949 align:start size:94% position:6%
Up next, Cara Santa Maria
goes behind the scenes of the

00:31:59.049 --> 00:32:02.520 align:start size:88% position:13%
global meteorite
market, in North Africa.

00:32:07.525 --> 00:32:08.692
CARA: I'm Cara Santa Maria,

00:32:08.792 --> 00:32:10.862
and here in Morocco when a
meteorite strikes the Earth,

00:32:10.962 --> 00:32:13.998 align:start size:88% position:13%
there's a rush
to find the best pieces.

00:32:14.899 --> 00:32:17.034
From scientists to
private collectors,

00:32:17.134 --> 00:32:19.937
everyone's interested in the
secrets of the cosmos that

00:32:20.037 --> 00:32:22.740
these rocks could unlock.

00:32:29.079 --> 00:32:31.782
Many meteorites are unchanged
since the birth of our solar

00:32:31.882 --> 00:32:34.452 align:start size:91% position:9%
system, and can tell
scientists a lot about our

00:32:34.552 --> 00:32:37.855
place in the universe.

00:32:41.826 --> 00:32:44.828 align:start size:94% position:6%
More than 40 tons of
interplanetary rocks and dust

00:32:44.928 --> 00:32:47.965 align:start size:81% position:19%
fall through our
atmosphere every day.

00:32:48.065 --> 00:32:51.568 align:start size:88% position:13%
Most are lost to
oceans or dense terrain.

00:32:51.935 --> 00:32:54.171
But of the precious few
meteorites that have been

00:32:54.271 --> 00:32:57.608 align:start size:81% position:19%
found, nearly one
in five landed here.

00:33:41.352 --> 00:33:44.088 align:start size:91% position:9%
CARA: In the oasis town of
Erfoud, Morocco a large

00:33:44.188 --> 00:33:47.391
percentage of the economy
is made up in meteorites.

00:33:47.491 --> 00:33:49.093 align:start size:78% position:22%
Known as the
gate of the Sahara,

00:33:49.193 --> 00:33:50.961
people come from all over
the world to trade in this

00:33:51.061 --> 00:33:53.398
precious commodity.

00:33:54.965 --> 00:33:57.301
Michael Farmer is a
meteorite dealer,

00:33:57.401 --> 00:33:59.870 align:start size:94% position:6%
one of only 20 people in
the world with access to the

00:33:59.970 --> 00:34:02.540
nearly impenetrable
meteorite market.

00:34:05.710 --> 00:34:08.479
(speaking native language).

00:34:09.447 --> 00:34:10.881
CARA: These guys
you trade with?

00:34:10.981 --> 00:34:12.683
MICHAEL: Yes, yeah.
CARA: Yeah? Cool.

00:34:12.783 --> 00:34:13.851
MICHAEL: I've known
them a lot of years.

00:34:13.951 --> 00:34:16.219
CARA: Really? Nice. Aw.

00:34:16.320 --> 00:34:17.888
MICHAEL: Dates!
Mm. Shukran.

00:34:17.988 --> 00:34:19.389 align:start size:78% position:22%
CARA: This is your
full-time job?

00:34:19.490 --> 00:34:21.425 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
MICHAEL: I worked at
Target while I was in the
University of Arizona,

00:34:21.525 --> 00:34:24.695 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
and, uh, I quit that job,
traveled to Morocco,

00:34:24.795 --> 00:34:26.731 line:0%
started buying meteorites,
and I've never looked back.

00:34:26.831 --> 00:34:28.599
CARA: Do you travel the world
in search of these things?

00:34:28.699 --> 00:34:30.300 align:start size:91% position:9%
MICHAEL: Yeah, I've
been to over 80 countries.

00:34:30.400 --> 00:34:31.669
I, I never stop.

00:34:31.769 --> 00:34:33.304
Any time a new
meteorite falls,

00:34:33.404 --> 00:34:36.174
uh, I tend to jump on a
plane and rush there.

00:34:48.352 --> 00:34:51.923 align:start size:91% position:9%
CARA: Michael has a network
of meteorite brokers all
around the world.

00:34:52.023 --> 00:34:55.059 align:start size:88% position:13%
Here in Morocco, his
local agent is Mohammed.

00:34:55.159 --> 00:34:57.561
In anticipation of
Michael's arrival,

00:34:57.661 --> 00:35:00.998
he has set up secret
meetings with a small
group of top sellers.

00:35:01.866 --> 00:35:04.001
Thank you.

00:35:05.836 --> 00:35:08.172
The one we're about to meet
claims that he has two rare

00:35:08.272 --> 00:35:12.210
rocks out of only 200 of its
kind that were ever found.

00:35:16.580 --> 00:35:18.515
MICHAEL: The rarest
Mars rock on earth.

00:35:18.615 --> 00:35:20.317 align:start size:78% position:22%
Scientifically,
it's extraordinary.

00:35:20.418 --> 00:35:22.853
It has a lot of water
in it, Martian water.

00:35:22.953 --> 00:35:26.624 align:start size:81% position:19%
Usually sells for
10,000 U.S. per gram.

00:35:27.724 --> 00:35:29.693
Yeah, no doubts.

00:35:29.793 --> 00:35:32.897
Yeah, very, very,
very rare rock.

00:35:33.464 --> 00:35:35.967
Gold is $50 a gram.

00:35:36.067 --> 00:35:38.903
Just those two pieces
they want $20,000.

00:35:39.303 --> 00:35:40.871 align:start size:94% position:6%
CARA: $20,000 for
what's in my hand right now?

00:35:40.971 --> 00:35:43.507
MICHAEL: Yes.

00:35:43.607 --> 00:35:46.377 align:start size:84% position:16%
CARA: The existence of
this global underground
meteorite industry

00:35:46.477 --> 00:35:49.513
is actually a huge problem
according to another group of

00:35:49.613 --> 00:35:52.984
people desperate to get their
hands on these rare rocks.

00:35:58.822 --> 00:36:01.859
Paul Chodas is the head of
Near Earth Objects for NASA,

00:36:01.959 --> 00:36:04.694
and believes meteorites are
too important to be collected

00:36:04.795 --> 00:36:07.865
by anyone other
than scientists.

00:36:07.965 --> 00:36:11.235
PAUL: Oh, a meteorite is a stone
that is sitting on the ground.

00:36:11.569 --> 00:36:15.039 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
Uh, a stone from
space, a rock from space.

00:36:16.707 --> 00:36:19.109
It's exciting because it
formed in space 4 billion

00:36:19.209 --> 00:36:22.146
years ago, so we can learn
more about the formation of

00:36:22.246 --> 00:36:24.649
the solar system, and the
composition of that early

00:36:24.749 --> 00:36:28.085 align:start position:0%
material that was, were the
building blocks of the planets.

00:36:31.255 --> 00:36:33.557
So, to ascertain,
kind of the age,

00:36:33.657 --> 00:36:36.327
and when the fall happened,
and where there are other

00:36:36.427 --> 00:36:39.063 align:start size:91% position:9%
related meteorites,
documentation is certainly

00:36:39.163 --> 00:36:41.799 align:start size:88% position:13%
needed when
meteorites are recovered.

00:36:41.899 --> 00:36:44.702 align:start position:0%
And I, I can't speak to what the
meteorite hunters are doing.

00:36:46.771 --> 00:36:50.007 align:start size:81% position:19%
CARA: Scientists like
Paul have good reason
to be suspicious.

00:36:50.107 --> 00:36:52.676 align:start size:97% position:3%
In an underground marketplace,
even well-known meteorite

00:36:52.776 --> 00:36:56.080
hunters sometimes struggle to
get the information they need.

00:36:56.914 --> 00:37:00.551
(speaking native language).

00:37:02.252 --> 00:37:04.221 align:start size:88% position:13%
CARA: Should we take off
our shoes here? Okay.

00:37:04.321 --> 00:37:05.823
We sit here? Here?

00:37:05.923 --> 00:37:07.257
MOHAMED: Yeah.
CARA: Oh, here.

00:37:07.357 --> 00:37:09.593 align:start size:84% position:16%
MAN: Anywhere you want.
Grab a pillow.

00:37:09.693 --> 00:37:11.595
CARA: Oh, thank you.

00:37:11.695 --> 00:37:13.331 align:start size:81% position:19%
MOHAMED: Wow.
MICHAEL: What's this?

00:37:15.499 --> 00:37:16.834 align:start size:81% position:19%
MICHAEL: Yeah,
that's a nice piece.

00:37:16.934 --> 00:37:19.770
Wow. Look at that.

00:37:19.870 --> 00:37:22.339
A very nice oriented rock.

00:37:22.439 --> 00:37:24.942
CARA: Chondrites are pieces of
asteroid and are thought to be

00:37:25.042 --> 00:37:28.311
one of the earliest building
blocks of our solar system.

00:37:29.045 --> 00:37:32.183 align:start size:94% position:6%
Nearly 90% of meteorites
found so far are chondrites,

00:37:32.783 --> 00:37:35.286 align:start size:94% position:6%
and researchers consider
the specific details of each

00:37:35.386 --> 00:37:38.389 align:start size:97% position:3%
rock's fall to the earth vital
to the scientific record.

00:37:38.889 --> 00:37:42.393
But this dealer is being cryptic
about where he found this one.

00:37:52.069 --> 00:37:53.971
MICHAEL: I saw pieces of
this a few months ago.

00:37:54.071 --> 00:37:56.473 align:start size:78% position:22%
It looks like a
fall from Algeria.

00:37:57.407 --> 00:38:00.344 align:start size:94% position:6%
CARA: It's illegal to export
meteorites from Algeria.

00:38:00.444 --> 00:38:02.145 align:start size:81% position:19%
They're seen as
priceless artifacts,

00:38:02.245 --> 00:38:04.014
and property of the state.

00:38:04.114 --> 00:38:05.783
So you're concerned about
these coming from a place

00:38:05.883 --> 00:38:07.718 align:start size:78% position:22%
where it's not
legal to sell them?

00:38:07.818 --> 00:38:09.553 align:start size:81% position:19%
MICHAEL: Yeah. If
I had information on,

00:38:09.653 --> 00:38:12.423 align:start size:88% position:13%
on that exactly I
would buy it. But, uh...

00:38:32.910 --> 00:38:36.314
CARA: Morocco is the global hub
of a booming meteorite market.

00:38:39.349 --> 00:38:42.486
But these keys to the origins
of the cosmos are also vital

00:38:42.586 --> 00:38:45.589
to science, and it seems
some dealers are willing to

00:38:45.689 --> 00:38:49.160 align:start size:97% position:3%
compromise critical scientific
data just to make a profit.

00:39:00.871 --> 00:39:02.106
MICHAEL: If I had
information on,

00:39:02.206 --> 00:39:04.542 align:start size:84% position:16%
on that exactly I would
buy it. But, uh.

00:39:16.653 --> 00:39:18.322
CARA: So you're concerned
about these coming from a

00:39:18.422 --> 00:39:20.357
place where it's not
legal to sell them?

00:39:20.457 --> 00:39:21.825 align:start size:81% position:19%
MICHAEL: Yeah.
At the moment, with,

00:39:21.926 --> 00:39:23.861
with the information I
have, I won't buy it.

00:39:23.961 --> 00:39:25.962
I would love to buy it, but I
don't like what I'm hearing,

00:39:26.062 --> 00:39:28.332
so I, I'm out.

00:39:28.799 --> 00:39:31.168
CARA: Michael may have
turned this rock down,

00:39:31.268 --> 00:39:33.370
but some collectors
look the other way,

00:39:33.470 --> 00:39:36.039
destroying critical data
scientists need to create a

00:39:36.139 --> 00:39:38.809
complete picture of
a meteorite fall.

00:39:39.310 --> 00:39:41.345 align:start size:81% position:19%
PAUL: There often
is a "strewn field",

00:39:41.445 --> 00:39:44.181 line:0%
that is to say, there are
um, a bunch of meteorites

00:39:44.281 --> 00:39:46.049 line:0%
that are related, together.

00:39:46.149 --> 00:39:48.719 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
You know, and, and that
mapping out that field is also

00:39:48.819 --> 00:39:50.788 line:0%
of great interest.

00:39:52.189 --> 00:39:54.558
If the local citizens have
picked up a meteorite and

00:39:54.658 --> 00:39:57.228 align:start size:88% position:13%
moved it, you know,
hundreds of miles maybe,

00:39:57.328 --> 00:40:00.730 align:start position:0%
then, then we have no
information on where that fell.

00:40:01.064 --> 00:40:03.700 align:start size:94% position:6%
So, in general, my preference
is that professionals are

00:40:03.800 --> 00:40:06.837 align:start size:91% position:9%
involved in uh,
recovering the meteorites.

00:40:12.676 --> 00:40:15.479
CARA: Michael Farmer argues
that despite some bad actors,

00:40:15.579 --> 00:40:18.382 align:start size:97% position:3%
the vast majority of meteorite
hunters are careful and

00:40:18.482 --> 00:40:21.652
coordinate with scientists.

00:40:21.752 --> 00:40:23.486
MICHAEL: Any time I
get a new meteorite,

00:40:23.587 --> 00:40:26.157
we have to share
20 grams or 20%.

00:40:26.257 --> 00:40:28.992 align:start size:91% position:9%
And that's been a joint
agreement with collectors,

00:40:29.092 --> 00:40:32.429
dealers, and, uh, scientists,
to make sure that science gets

00:40:32.529 --> 00:40:35.966 align:start size:84% position:16%
its share, or at
least enough to study.

00:40:38.835 --> 00:40:41.338
CARA: But it's not just out of
the goodness of their hearts,

00:40:41.438 --> 00:40:44.274 align:start size:91% position:9%
in a twist of irony,
scientific certification is

00:40:44.374 --> 00:40:47.111
actually the best way for
meteorite traders to get

00:40:47.211 --> 00:40:50.047
top dollar for their finds.

00:40:55.219 --> 00:40:57.521
HYSLOP: I have heard stories
of meteorites changing hands

00:40:57.621 --> 00:41:00.657 line:0%
for $1 million, or
in excess of it.

00:41:01.525 --> 00:41:03.794 align:start size:91% position:9%
CARA: James Hyslop sources
the most extraordinary

00:41:03.894 --> 00:41:06.029
meteorites on the planet.

00:41:06.129 --> 00:41:08.966
HYSLOP: I'm really looking
for the best of the best.

00:41:09.066 --> 00:41:13.203 align:start size:94% position:6%
I'd say for every 100
meteorites that I'm offered,

00:41:13.303 --> 00:41:16.941 align:start size:81% position:19%
I probably turn
down 98, 99 of them.

00:41:21.311 --> 00:41:23.547
CARA: He's currently receiving
deliveries from brokers around

00:41:23.647 --> 00:41:26.750 align:start size:97% position:3%
the world for one of
Christie's meteorite auctions.

00:41:27.985 --> 00:41:29.853
HYSLOP: Wow.

00:41:29.953 --> 00:41:32.956
DARRYL: Canyon Diablo, named
after meteor crater in Arizona.

00:41:33.790 --> 00:41:38.229 line:0%
Most, as you know, most Canyon
Diablo's do not look like this.

00:41:39.496 --> 00:41:40.697
HYSLOP: No.

00:41:40.797 --> 00:41:42.900
CARA: And when you're dropping
the same amount of cash on a

00:41:43.000 --> 00:41:45.302
piece of rock as
you would a home,

00:41:45.402 --> 00:41:48.238 align:start size:97% position:3%
like a home, you want
qualified experts to make sure

00:41:48.338 --> 00:41:50.474
you're buying what
you think you are.

00:41:50.574 --> 00:41:52.910 align:start size:91% position:9%
HYSLOP: Until it's been
formally classified by the

00:41:53.010 --> 00:41:56.046 align:start size:81% position:19%
Meteoritical Society,
it's just a rock.

00:41:56.146 --> 00:41:59.149 align:start size:94% position:6%
So it, it then has to,
basically has to be cut open,

00:41:59.249 --> 00:42:03.653 align:start size:97% position:3%
analyzed, published, then it's
officially a meteorite and

00:42:03.754 --> 00:42:06.724 align:start size:84% position:16%
then we'll offer it
for sale at Christie's.

00:42:07.725 --> 00:42:09.960 align:start size:94% position:6%
This really has the potential
to be the most expensive

00:42:10.060 --> 00:42:12.329
meteorite we've ever
sold at Christie's.

00:42:12.429 --> 00:42:14.297 align:start size:78% position:22%
DARRYL: Uh, it
should be. It's a.

00:42:14.398 --> 00:42:15.999 align:start size:94% position:6%
HYSLOP: Yeah.
DARRYL: It's a one-of-a-kind.

00:42:16.099 --> 00:42:17.500 align:start size:84% position:16%
I mean they're all
one-of-a-kind objects,

00:42:17.600 --> 00:42:19.235
but this is extraordinary.

00:42:19.336 --> 00:42:21.672
This is really
is extraordinary.

00:42:21.772 --> 00:42:25.041 align:start position:0%
CARA: At the auction Christie's
held in February 2018,

00:42:25.275 --> 00:42:28.512 align:start size:94% position:6%
this scientifically-certified
Canyon Diablo meteorite

00:42:28.612 --> 00:42:33.083
sold for a staggering $237,500.

00:42:35.619 --> 00:42:38.355
So, in many ways, scientists
help fuel the high prices in

00:42:38.455 --> 00:42:40.190
the meteorite market.

00:42:40.290 --> 00:42:42.693
But they also get
a lot in return.

00:42:48.698 --> 00:42:51.435
Recovering these incredible
pieces of outer space from

00:42:51.535 --> 00:42:55.572
far-flung regions is a costly
and time-consuming endeavor

00:42:56.640 --> 00:42:59.176 align:start size:94% position:6%
and scientists almost
never have to foot the bill,

00:42:59.276 --> 00:43:01.711 align:start size:94% position:6%
because the meteorite hunters
are chasing a much bigger

00:43:01.811 --> 00:43:04.815 align:start size:91% position:9%
payday than any government
budget would provide.

00:43:05.215 --> 00:43:07.017 align:start size:88% position:13%
MICHAEL: Okay.
Now that's a nice piece.

00:43:07.117 --> 00:43:09.954
Nice moon rock, big moon rock.

00:43:10.955 --> 00:43:12.790
I mean, I jump on a
plane, I go to India,

00:43:12.890 --> 00:43:15.192 align:start size:97% position:3%
I get the meteorite, and I
deliver it to scientific hands

00:43:15.292 --> 00:43:17.394
in 72 hours.

00:43:18.562 --> 00:43:21.298 align:start size:97% position:3%
Whereas, they may have to
write up a budget proposal and

00:43:21.398 --> 00:43:24.267 align:start size:97% position:3%
wait a year on somebody to
give them some money to go and

00:43:24.367 --> 00:43:27.404 align:start size:88% position:13%
try to acquire a sample,
we cut that red tape.

00:43:32.910 --> 00:43:34.945 align:start size:88% position:13%
MICHAEL: That's $15,000.
Fourteen nine.

00:43:35.045 --> 00:43:36.180 line:0%
MOHAMED: Yeah.

00:43:37.914 --> 00:43:40.084 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
CARA: After cutting, polishing
and obtaining scientific

00:43:40.184 --> 00:43:44.487 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
certification, Michael
could clear at least $45,000

00:43:44.587 --> 00:43:48.158 line:0%
in profit from this one and a
half pound piece of the moon.

00:43:48.558 --> 00:43:51.362 line:0%
MICHAEL: He's happy, I'm
happy, everybody's happy.

00:43:52.429 --> 00:43:56.132 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
PHIL: In this episode,
we've seen how enduring
human curiosity is

00:43:56.232 --> 00:43:59.035 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
when it comes to understanding
how the universe works.

00:43:59.603 --> 00:44:02.338 line:0%
Many people believe our
long-term survival as a

00:44:02.438 --> 00:44:04.708 line:0%
species will depend on it.

00:44:04.808 --> 00:44:07.578 align:start position:0% line:0%
That's it for this episode.
Join me next time, on<i> Explorer.</i>

00:44:07.678 --> 00:44:08.846 line:0%
Captioned by Cotter
Captioning Services.

